844:
(a) Every applicant for a license to be an operator of a massage establishment shall file an application under oath with the department upon a form provided by the department. An annual license fee in such amount as provided in this division shall be tendered with the application. Copies of the application shall be forwarded to the building inspection department, the fire department and police department. These departments shall within 30 days inspect any premises proposed to be operated as a massage establishment and shall make written verification to the department concerning compliance with applicable codes.
(b) Each application shall contain the following information:
(1) The name and addresses for the past five years of the applicant and each person who would have a beneficial interest in the license. However, if the applicant is a corporation, only the names and addresses of the registered agents and those stockholders owning at least five percent of the stock must be disclosed;
(2) The name of the business operation, including a description of all services to be provided;
(3) The location, mailing address and telephone number where the business is to be conducted;
(4) The name, address, social security number and date of birth of each employee working at the massage establishment. Changes in the list of employees with the names of new employees, together with all information relating thereto, and all exhibits must be filed with the department within three days from the date of change;
(5) Business, occupation or employment of the applicant for the three years immediately preceding the date of application;
(6) The massage or similarly related business license history of the applicant; whether such person or entity has had a business license revoked or suspended in this or another county or state, and if so, the reason therefor, and the business activity or occupation of the applicant subsequent to such action of suspension or revocation;
(7) All criminal convictions other than misdemeanor traffic violations, including the dates and place of conviction, and nature of the offense for all those persons named in response to number (1) of this subsection (b), as well as a listing of all violations of any laws and criminal convictions of persons while an employee, independent contractor, or agent of the applicant, for all offenses other than misdemeanor traffic violations, including the dates and place of violation or conviction, and nature of the offense;
For the purpose of this subsection, a conviction shall include a finding or verdict of guilty, plea of guilty, or a plea of nolo contendere in a criminal proceeding regardless of whether the adjudication of guilt or sentence is withheld or not entered thereon pursuant to provisions of O.C.G.A. §§ 42-8-60 through 42-8-64, relating to first offenders, or any comparable rule or statute;
(8) The name, address and description of any massage business or other establishment owned or operated by a person whose name is required to be given by this article; and
(9) A description of any other business to be operated on the same premises or on adjoining premises owned or controlled by the applicant.
(c) The applicant shall submit with the application certain documentation as required by the department, including but not limited to the following:
(1) A blueprint or a scale drawing of the establishment;
(2) Proof that the applicant and all employees providing massage services are at least 18 years of age;
(3) For any applicant who is to provide massage services and each person who is an independent contractor, employee, or agent of applicant who is to provide massage services, a copy of that person's diploma or certificate of graduation from an educational program or course of study of not less than 500 hours in duration from a school which is licensed by the State of Georgia or one of the other 49 states, which school is accredited by an accrediting agency for professional massage and the accrediting agency is recognized by the United States Department of Education, or if applicable, a copy of the affidavits and the examination results provided for in section 22-137(a)(2). However, if the applicant will not engage in the practice of massage, such applicant need not provide a diploma or certificate of graduation, or results of the examination and affidavits;
(4) A signed and notarized consent form authorizing the department and the police department, their respective agents and employees to seek information and conduct a background check on the applicant and all employees (in the case of a massage establishment), and to conduct an investigation into the truth of the statements set forth in the application and the qualifications of the applicant for a license;
(5) A signed and notarized consent form authorizing the fire department, their respective agents and employees to inspect the premises to insure compliance with the fire safety code, all other codes and laws under their jurisdiction, and applicable provisions of this article;
(6) A current health certificate from a physician on the applicant who is to provide massage services, each person who is an independent contractor, employee, or agent of applicant who is to provide massage services, and all employee trainees providing massage services indicating that they do not have a communicable disease or otherwise would not present a threat to the health, safety and welfare of their respective clientele. However, if the applicant will not engage in the practice of massage, such applicant need not provide such health certificate; and
(7) A copy of this article signed by the applicant and all persons who are independent contractors, employees, or agents of applicant who are to provide massage services and all employee trainees providing massage services acknowledging that each has read and understands the provisions contained therein.
(d) (1) Every applicant for a license to engage in the practice as a massage therapist shall file an application under oath with the department upon a form provided by the department. An annual license fee in such amount as provided in this division shall be tendered with the application. A copy of the application shall be forwarded to the police department, which shall have 30 days to make written verification to the department concerning compliance with applicable provisions of this article.
(2) Each applicant shall submit with the application certain documentation as required by the department, including but no limited to the following:
a. A copy of applicant's diploma or certificate of graduation from an educational program or course of study of not less than 500 hours in duration from a school which is licensed by the State of Georgia or one of the other 49 states, which school is accredited by an accrediting agency for professional massage and the accrediting agency is recognized by the United States Department of Education provided for in section 22-137(a)(1), or, if applicable, a copy of the affidavits and the examination results provided for in section 22-137(a)(2);
b. A signed and notarized consent form authorizing the business license division and the police department, their respective agents and employees to seek information and conduct a background check on the applicant and to conduct an investigation into the truth of the statements set forth in the application and the qualifications of the applicant for a license;
c. A current health certificate from a physician on the applicant indicating that the applicant does not have a communicable disease or otherwise would not present a threat to the health, safety and welfare of his or her clientele; and
d. A copy of this article signed by the applicant acknowledging that he or she has read and understands the provisions contained therein.
(Ord. No. 93-4, § II(2-16-131), 2-16-93; Ord. No. 2003-32, §§ 3—6, 3-18-03)

846:
(a) The department shall have authority to refuse to grant a license to an applicant or to discipline a licensee under this article upon a finding by the department that the licensee or applicant has:
(1) Displayed an inability or has become unable to work as a massage therapist or as an operator of a massage establishment, as the case may be, with reasonable skill and safety to clientele by reason of illness, use of alcohol, drugs, narcotics, chemicals, or any other type of substance, or as a result of any mental or physical condition. In enforcing this subsection, the department may, upon reasonable grounds, require the licensee or applicant to submit to a mental or physical examination by an appropriate practitioner of the healing arts. The expense of such examination shall be borne by such licensee or applicant. The results of such examination shall be admissible in any hearing before the department, the board of commissioners, or a court of competent jurisdiction, in the event of an appeal. If a licensee or applicant fails to submit to such an examination when properly directed to do so by the department, unless such failure was due to circumstances beyond the licensee's or applicant's control, the department may enter a final order upon proper notice, hearing, and proof of such refusal;
(2) Been convicted in the last ten years of any offenses involving the use of or threat of force or violence upon the person of another; any offenses involving narcotics, dangerous drugs, or dangerous weapons; any felony involving moral turpitude; any offenses involving sexual behavior, whether felony or misdemeanor, as set out in O.C.G.A. Title 16, Crimes and Offenses, Chapter 6, Sexual Offenses. For the purpose of this subsection, a conviction shall include a finding or verdict of guilty, plea of guilty, or a plea of nolo contendere in a criminal proceeding regardless of whether the adjudication of guilt or sentence is withheld or not entered thereon pursuant to provisions of O.C.G.A. §§ 42-8-60 through 42-8-64, relating to first offenders, or any comparable rule or statute;
(3) Knowingly made misleading, deceptive, untrue, or fraudulent representations to a client, customer, or other person or entity in connection with the provisions of massage services or any document connected therewith; or practiced fraud or deceit or intentionally made any false statement in obtaining or attempting to obtain a license provided for in this article;
(4) Performed massage services contrary to this article; or knowingly aided, assisted, procured, or advised any person to perform massage services contrary to this article; or knowingly performed any act which in any way aids, assists, procures, advises, or encourages any unlicensed person to perform massage services;
(5) Engaged in any unprofessional, unethical, deceptive, deleterious practice harmful to the public, which conduct or practice need not have resulted in actual injury to any person; unprofessional conduct shall also include any departure from or failure to conform to the acceptable and prevailing minimal standards applicable to massage therapists or operators of massage establishments; or
(6) Failed to report to the department any act or omission of a licensee or applicant or any other person who violates the provisions of this article.
(7) In regard to a license to operate a massage establishment, had more than one occurrence within the past ten years of any of the license holder's employees, independent contractors, or agents having violated or been convicted of offenses involving the use of or threat of force or violence upon the person of another, any offenses involving narcotics, dangerous drugs, or dangerous weapons, any felony involving moral turpitude or any offenses involving sexual behavior, whether felony or misdemeanor, as set out in O.C.G.A., Title 16, Crimes and Offenses, Chapter 6, Sexual Offenses, which arose from acts or behavior on the premises of a massage establishment. For the purpose of this subsection, a conviction shall include a finding or verdict of guilty, plea of guilty, or a plea of nolo contendere in a criminal proceeding regardless of whether the adjudication of guilt or sentence is withheld or not entered thereon pursuant to provisions of O.C.G.A. §§ 42-8-60 through 42-8-64, relating to first offenders, or any comparable rule or statute.
(b) When the department finds that any person is unqualified to be granted a license or finds that any person should be disciplined pursuant to subsection (a) of this section, the department may take such action as more fully set forth in section 22-34.
(c) For purposes of this section, a licensee or applicant, whether the same is a person, partnership, firm, corporation or other entity, who may or may not perform massage services, and all persons having a beneficial interest in the operation of a massage establishment (except stockholders owning less than five percent of the stock in a corporation) shall be subject to the provisions of this section and be treated as if they were the licensee or applicant.
(d) Where the department refused to grant a license to an applicant or the county has revoked a license under this article, the effected licensee or applicant shall be disqualified from reapplying for such license for 12 months immediately following such action taken by the department.
(Ord. No. 93-4, § II(2-16-138), 2-16-93; Ord. No. 2003-32, §§ 8, 9, 3-18-03; Ord. No. 2019-51, § 4, 5-7-19)

